In Linux, you can create a child process using the `fork()` system call. Here's a simple example in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    pid_t pid;

    pid = fork();

    if (pid == 0) {
        // Child process
        printf("Child process: I am running\n");
        // Add your child process code here
    } else if (pid > 0) {
        // Parent process
        printf("Parent process: Child process %d exited\n", pid);
        // Add your parent process code here
    } else {
        // Error: fork() failed
        perror("fork");
        return 1;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This program creates a child process using `fork()`. The child process runs independently, and the parent process waits for the child to finish before continuing.
